==Allegations of vote manipulation==
Although Preval's vote count was initially over 60% of the total—in an election marred by declarations of invalid votes, allegations of fraud, errors and the discovery of perhaps thousands of ballots dumped and burned in Port-au-Prince—election officials of the interim government ordered a halt to the publication of full election results pending an inquiry into possible electoral fraud.<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk">{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/americas/4713802.stm|title = Haiti bars release of poll result|date = 15 February 2006}}</ref>

As the provisional electoral council announced that Preval's vote count had slipped below the 50% required to avoid a second round of voting, thousands of his supporters marched through Port-au-Prince in protest at what they claimed was an effort to manipulate the vote count and suppress support for Preval. At least two of the nine electoral council members, Pierre Richard Duchemin and Patrick Fequiere, have also alleged that the vote tabulation was being manipulated.<ref>[https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/tensions-high-in-haiti-election-official-wants-investigation-1.574149 http://www.alertnet.org/thenews/newsdesk/N13263370.htm]</ref>

Of the 2.2 million ballots cast, roughly 125,000 were declared invalid. A further 4% of the ballots were blank but were nonetheless added to the count, thereby lowering the percentage of the vote a candidate receives.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/02/14/ap/world/mainD8FP50IGA.shtml |title=CBS News |website=[[CBS News]] |access-date=2006-02-15 |archive-date=2006-02-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060223030633/http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/02/14/ap/world/mainD8FP50IGA.shtml |url-status=dead }}</ref>

During the protests, at least one man was killed and many others wounded during clashes between U.N. forces and Preval supporters. Witnesses claimed that Jordanian soldiers, who are serving as part of MINUSTAH, shot at the protesters. U.N. spokesman David Wimhurst at first denied that the U.N. soldiers had fired any shots, but then later said that the soldiers fired two "warning shots" into the air.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/02/13/world/main1311869_page2.shtml |title=Haiti Election Results Spur Violence - CBS News |website=[[CBS News]] |access-date=2006-02-15 |archive-date=2011-05-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523231720/http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/02/13/world/main1311869_page2.shtml |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Talking publicly for the first time since the voting took place, Preval said "We are convinced there was massive fraud and gross errors that affected the process,". Soon after, many hundreds (possibly thousands) of charred ballots were found in a Port-au-Prince garbage dump.<ref>http://today.reuters.co.uk/news/newsArticle.aspx?type=worldNews&storyID=2006-02-15T040820Z_01_N14355245_RTRUKOC_0_UK-HAITI-ELECTION.xml&archived=False {{dead link|date=July 2021|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

On February 14, 2006, the interim government ordered a halt to the publication of full election results pending an inquiry into the alleged electoral fraud.<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk"/>

